From 12e1de4671975a9fab1f047ea775c2e49e75307a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 00:36:10 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 16/30] Add VSX paths for text scanning on PowerPC

CharacterDataBuffer::FirstNon8Bit decides whether text node data can be stored
as Latin1. The PowerPC version used aligned VMX loads, so it walked up to seven
characters with scalar code to reach 16-byte alignment and then tested one
vector at a time with a record-form compare and a branch.

When the target baseline has VSX, use unaligned loads instead: no alignment
prologue, four vectors OR-ed together and tested once per 32 characters, and
the last partial block covered by one overlapping load of the final eight
characters. The function only has to return a lower bound of the first
non-8-bit index, which the block start is. The VMX version stays for targets
without VSX in their baseline (PowerPC 970, big-endian distributions with a
POWER4 baseline), selected by the PPC_TARGET_HAS_VSX probe.

mozglue's SIMD string search helpers get the same treatment.

SIMD::memchr8/16/32/64 and memchr2x8/2x16 back String.prototype.indexOf,
includes and split for one- and two-character needles in both the
interpreter and Ion, and TypedArray and Array indexOf. They had SSE2,
AVX2, NEON and LSX implementations and fell back to a scalar loop, or to
libc memchr for 8-bit lanes, on PowerPC.

The VSX implementation follows the SSE2 one, built on unaligned vec_xl
loads. The compile-time baseline picks how the first match is located:
POWER9 and POWER10 use vclzlsbb, POWER8 gathers the top bit of each
compared byte into a GPR mask with vbpermq, and VSX targets without the
POWER8 vector extensions store the compare result and scan it with
cntlzd or cnttzd. 64-bit lanes, used by Array.prototype.indexOf on
Values, are vectorized where the POWER8 extensions are available. Both
endiannesses are handled: lane 0 holds the lowest address either way,
but that address is the least significant byte of a 64-bit lane on LE
and the most significant on BE, and vec_sld moves bytes in opposite
directions.

GCC on little-endian POWER8 selects lvx for loads it can prove aligned
and then spills every vector through the stack to recover memory order
for the index extraction, so an empty asm statement hides the alignment
from it after the align-up. 8-bit searches over 512 bytes or more go to
glibc's memchr, whose hand-written POWER8 version is faster than this
loop on long inputs; the vector code keeps the short cases, where it
beats glibc's call and dispatch overhead.

On a POWER10 with clang, memchr16 over 4096 code units goes from 2 GB/s
to 50 GB/s, and the two-character 8-bit search, which used to call
memchr once per candidate, from 0.16 GB/s to 13 GB/s.
